# Migrating `EventProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Issues with the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed in .NET 7+
3. **Race condition**: multiple enqueues with single `WaitOne`/`Reset` can lose items
4. **No graceful shutdown** mechanism

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _processingTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Failed to write to channel.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete(); // signals no more items
        _cts?.Cancel();            // fallback if reader is idle

        var waitTime = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_processingTask is not null)
            await _processingTask.WaitAsync(waitTime);
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // Your processing logic here
    }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. Use `CancellationToken` instead. |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Removed in .NET 7+. Same fix — use cancellation. |
| `ManualResetEvent` race | Multiple signals before `Reset()` loses items. `Channel<T>` handles this correctly. |
| Blocking threads | Wastes thread-pool resources. `async/await` with channels is non-blocking. |
| No backpressure | Use `CreateBounded<T>(capacity)` if you need to throttle producers. |

## If You Need a Synchronous API (drop-in replacement)

```csharp
public class EventProcessorSync : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop) { IsBackground = true };
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
                Handle(item);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _queue.CompleteAdding();       // drain remaining items
        _cts.Cancel();                 // interrupt wait immediately
        _thread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _queue.Dispose();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { }
}
```

## Summary

- **Prefer `Channel<T>`** for async producer/consumer (best performance, backpressure support).
- **Use `BlockingCollection<T>`** if you must keep synchronous thread semantics.
- **Replace all `Abort`/`Interrupt`** with `CancellationToken` — this is non-negotiable on .NET 8.
- **Always call `Complete()`/`CompleteAdding()`** to allow graceful drain on shutdown.
